## LEARNER RESPONSE CONTROLS (MANDATORY)
- Digitize every exercise or activity that asks the learner to answer, write, fill, complete, choose, name, identify, describe, match, draw, or say a response. Do not leave it as static textbook text or a screenshot.
- Put a dedicated editable control immediately beside, inside, or below the exact prompt it answers, matching the source-page relationship. Never collect answers in a detached area far from their questions.
- Use one control per distinct response. Picture-naming tasks require one text input below EACH picture; multi-question activities require one control per question; every printed blank requires an inline input at that blank.
- Use `<input type="text">` for a word or short phrase and `<textarea>` for a sentence, paragraph, explanation, drawing description, or multi-line response. Preserve oral/pair-work instructions, but still provide an optional response field so the activity is accessible to learners who cannot speak or hear.
- Every control must have a unique `id`, a unique sequential `data-activity-item="item-N"`, and an accessible name from an associated `<label>` or a specific `aria-label` containing its question/context. Controls must remain native keyboard-focusable and must not be disabled.
- Touch targets must be at least 44px high. Use `min-h-11`; use `w-full`/`flex-1` and responsive stacking so fields remain usable at 375px without clipping. In picture grids, put each image and its field in a `flex-col` container so the input appears BELOW the image and cannot collapse beside it.
- Editable controls are interactive additions and therefore MUST NOT carry `data-id`. Keep the exact source text in its provided `data-id` element and place the associated control outside that element.
- A crop or screenshot whose main content is a passage, worksheet, form, table, or exercise is NOT acceptable visible output. Recreate its text, word bank, borders, headings, and response areas as semantic HTML; do not display the text-bearing crop behind or above the reconstruction.
- When a passage contains blanks, keep the surrounding passage as one natural reading flow and replace each printed underline at its exact sentence position with an inline input. Never move passage blanks into a separate questionnaire or answer panel.
- Treat conversations and dialogues containing blanks as exercises: recreate every speaker turn as semantic HTML and put each response control at its printed blank. Never show a text-bearing conversation crop as the learner-facing activity.
- For word-bank or parenthesized-choice blanks, preserve the source mechanic with selectable answer chips and usable drop targets at the inline blanks. Also provide a keyboard-operable path (select a chip, focus a blank, then press Enter) plus direct typing or a native select/datalist fallback. Drag-and-drop MUST NOT be the only way to answer; give every target a specific accessible name and announce selection and placement through a live status region.
- For picture-based word-bank activities, put one labelled dropdown/drop target directly below each picture, give the picture meaningful alt text, and keep the reusable answer chips visible. Once a real control replaces a printed blank, remove the decorative underline/dash; never show both a control and a second empty line.
- Detect picture word-bank activities that continue onto an adjacent page. Digitize every continuation picture and repeat the complete choice bank on each rendered page so every page remains independently usable and accessible.
- Rebuild text-only tables and timetables as semantic HTML tables rather than visible screenshots. Use a caption plus scoped column/row headers, preserve every cell, and make the table wrap or reflow without horizontal clipping at 375px.
- If the crop also contains a genuine illustration, isolate and retain only that illustration when possible. The textual exercise and its controls must remain real HTML in normal flow and inside the visible page canvas.
